[
    {
        "content": "<p>While porting a private project of mine, I noticed that inferring <code>NonExpansive</code> by type class synthesis is incomplete (doesn't decompose pointful definitions) and potentially slow. </p>\n<p>I instructed Claude to implement a cheap tactic that does the compositional discharge that I know from the <code>monotonicity</code> tactic. It implemented a tactic <code>ne_solve</code> that works for my use case: <a href=\"https://github.com/sgraf812/abs-den-iris/blob/master/AbsDenIris/NonExpansive.lean\">https://github.com/sgraf812/abs-den-iris/blob/master/AbsDenIris/NonExpansive.lean</a><br>\nHowever, I don't think it is up to the standard in completeness and performance that we'd expect from Mathlib or iris-lean.</p>\n<p>Claude also pointed me at Mathlib's <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Tactic/FunProp.html\"><code>fun_prop</code></a> tactic, which sounds like a configurable generalization of <code>monotonicity</code> to me. I think using this tactic would be <em>very</em> useful for iris-lean! However, Mathlib is a heavy dependency. Maybe we should coordinate with <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"346070\">@Tomas Skrivan</span> (not yet subscribed to this channel) and ask him if he'd create a standalone package for this tactic? It sounds like a recurring itch that one wants to scratch.</p>",
        "id": 607414544,
        "sender_full_name": "Sebastian Graf",
        "timestamp": 1782834765
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In the end, my goal is to just write <code>ofe_fun x =&gt; e</code> instead of <code>fun x =&gt; e</code> and thus turn <code>α -&gt; β</code> into <code>α -n&gt; β</code> automatically. This is the macro:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Lean</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Parser</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">macro</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">max</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"ofe_fun\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">basicFun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Hom</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">basicFun</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ne_solve</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and this is the resulting shape of my interpreter:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">eval_simp</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Exp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Env</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"bp\">&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ref</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ofe_fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">get</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">getD</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stuck</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">lam</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">body</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">ofe_fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ofe_fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">step</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">app2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">body</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">]))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">app</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">ofe_fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">get</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">elim</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stuck</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">step</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">app1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">conapp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">xs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">ofe_fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">getMany</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">xs</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">elim</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stuck</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ds</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">con</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ds</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.«</span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"bp\">»</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">ofe_fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">step</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">let1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">bind</span>\n<span class=\"w\">          </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ofe_fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">step</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">look</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">])</span>\n<span class=\"w\">          </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ofe_fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">step</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">look</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">]))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">termination_by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sizeOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 607415181,
        "sender_full_name": "Sebastian Graf",
        "timestamp": 1782834938
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"432410\">@Arthur Adjedj</span> prototyped something of the sort in <a class=\"message-link\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/490604-iris-lean/topic/HeapLang.20sprint/near/598286641\">#iris-lean &gt; HeapLang sprint @ 💬</a>. I think that everyone agrees that this would be something we would like to have, but that rules have to be chosen carefully so they don't render a goal unsolvable by mistake</p>",
        "id": 607461658,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1782849236
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for the pointer! Indeed, <code>aseop_contractive</code> sounds pretty much like what <code>fun_prop</code> would do. The thing is: IIUC, <code>fun_prop</code> was specifically conceived to <em>replace</em> the existing <code>aesop</code>-based <code>continuity</code> etc. tactics. Note that <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Tactic/FunProp.html\">the <code>fun_prop</code> docs</a> has insight on how to \"carefully choose rules\", specifically the bit on \"Function theorems\" is insightful. </p>\n<p>I think a dependency on <code>fun_prop</code> (maybe under a better name ...) is much lighter than a dependency on <code>aesop</code> as well... But if in doubt, I think I'd prefer <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/pull/422\">iris-lean#422</a> over no tactic at all. Begs the question whether <code>NonExpansive</code> should be a <code>class</code>, though...</p>",
        "id": 607463353,
        "sender_full_name": "Sebastian Graf",
        "timestamp": 1782849867
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It's a good question. I think inference has been relatively poor so far.</p>",
        "id": 607464028,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1782850159
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yeap, I was going to say, I feel like most of the interesting instance rules are theorems so far, but is it because we hit performance issues if we make them instances?</p>",
        "id": 607464212,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1782850240
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Some instances were excluded to avoid diamonds</p>",
        "id": 607464300,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1782850273
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I see, the one that came to mind was <code>Iris.BI.forall_ne</code>. Do you happen to remember what the diamond with this one was?</p>",
        "id": 607464489,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1782850354
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Not off the top of my head, but I can look in a bit</p>",
        "id": 607464635,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1782850428
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><del>Hmm, wait, it's not even actually a <code>NonExpansive</code>, it needs quantification in its premise</del> I'd have to look into it too</p>",
        "id": 607464678,
        "sender_full_name": "Fernando LEAL",
        "timestamp": 1782850444
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I agree that having such a solve_ne tactic would be great. Arthurs MR is nice, but the aesop based tactic is quite slow. I asked Fable during the 3 glorious days to build a replacement and it created something that worked, but not quite as I liked. I never got around to fully finishing it. I am currently very busy, but I might get back in a few weeks. You can see the current (broken) state here: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/tree/solve_ne_tac\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/tree/solve_ne_tac</a></p>\n<p>But I think one basically wants to have the equivalent of the <code>monotonicity</code> tactic. (It would be great, if one could just repurpose <code>monotonicity</code> for this instead of having to reimplement it.)</p>",
        "id": 607465966,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Sammler",
        "timestamp": 1782850934
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>fun_prop</code> looks quite nice. I've not seen it before.</p>",
        "id": 607466406,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Sammler",
        "timestamp": 1782851080
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Not sure if type class synthesis can handle heads like <code>fun x =&gt; f (g x)</code>. It <em>could</em> handle <code>Function.comp f g</code>, but I don't want to write all my code point-free. That's one thing that <code>monotonicity</code>/<code>fun_prop</code> + annotations appears to solve. If instance synthesis can't handle <em>all</em> programs, I don't see a reason to build on it for <em>some</em> programs.</p>",
        "id": 607476668,
        "sender_full_name": "Sebastian Graf",
        "timestamp": 1782854949
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The example I was thinking of: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/iris-lean/blob/fd954b1d3968aa50a346f5a345e02ead77716285/Iris/Iris/Algebra/OFE.lean#L574\">nonexpansivity of the uncurry function</a>.</p>",
        "id": 607478445,
        "sender_full_name": "Markus de Medeiros",
        "timestamp": 1782855765
    }
]